SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Offsets: an apex of a face with a degenerated edge is cut only where its
construction has a solution; elsewhere that side is left as it is
(mitcad#2140, GitHub mitcad/Mitcad#517).

The case: a Y tube's crotch rounded (patch 2142), then shelled 2 mm
inwards with its ends open. Each rounding face ends in a point where the
crease fades (a degenerated edge at its vanishing end, ChFi3d_ShrinkVanishingEnd,
patch 0130). BRepOffset_Offset::Init takes a face with a degenerated edge
of an offset surface for one with an apex, like a cone's or a sphere's
pole, when the offset iso there is longer than 1e-5 (TolApex): it crosses
the generatrix with the apex line (through the degenerated point, square to
the plane of three points of the offset iso) and, offset inwards, projects
that crossing onto the opposite iso to cut the surface there. At the
rounding's vanishing end the offset iso is micrometres long and nearly
straight, the apex line has no meaning, and the crossing projects nowhere:
GeomAPI_ProjectPointOnCurve::LowerDistanceParameter read a solution that
does not exist and raised Standard_OutOfRange without a message, the thick
solid failing at once (MitCad: "the geometry kernel's offset raised an
error"; the debugger's stop: TKGeomAlgo under BRepOffset_Offset::Init from
MakeOffsetFaces).

- In each of the four sides (u and v, first and last), the apex is cut
  only where the generatrix crosses the apex line (GeomAPI_ExtremaCurveCurve
  has an extremum) and, offset inwards, the crossing projects onto the iso
  (GeomAPI_ProjectPointOnCurve has a point). Elsewhere that side is not
  cut and does not count as a singularity: the degenerated edge's offset is
  made as for any other edge (degenerated, at its vertex's offset). Where
  the construction has its solutions, nothing changes.
- A degenerated side is taken for an apex only where its offset iso is
  longer than 2 % of the offset (and than TolApex, 1e-5, as before): at
  an apex the surface's normals turn about the point, at a rounding's
  vanishing end they stay parallel. (With the guard alone, the projection
  on the rounding's other face found a meaningless point at the face's far
  end, the offset face was cut to nothing there, its edges' curves on it
  collapsed to a point, and the shell came out valid but with tolerances
  of tens of millimetres, every point classified on it.)
  The rounding's vanishing ends' offset isos measured 0.0045 (rounded
  with 6) and 0.0024 (with 50) for a wall of 2: a turn of the normals of
  about 0.1 degree; a cone's or a sphere's pole turns them by tens of
  degrees, its offset iso a sizable part of the offset (a first threshold
  of 1e-3 of the offset was below both).
- With MITCAD_TRACE_OFFSET_CREASE set, each degenerated side's offset iso
  length and the threshold are printed.
- (A face with two degenerated sides of which one is cut and the other not
  keeps the one singularity flag of the face, as before.)

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2143.
